17941:
Recall the field at the centre of a long solenoid as B = \mu_{0} n I where n = \frac{N}{L}
Convert length L = 50 cm = 0.50 m so n = \frac{100}{0.50} = 200 turns per metre
Insert \mu_{0} = 4\pi \times 10^{-7} T m A^{-1} and current I = 2.5 A
Compute B = 4\pi \times 10^{-7} \times 200 \times 2.5 = 20\pi \times 10^{-5}
Evaluate 20\pi \times 10^{-5} = 6.28 \times 10^{-4} T which matches the given option
→ (D)

17943:
Recall that in an electromagnetic wave the electric and magnetic energy densities are equal
Write time averaged densities u_{E} = \frac{1}{2} \varepsilon_{0} E_{0}^{2} and u_{B} = \frac{B_{0}^{2}}{2\mu_{0}}
Use B_{0} = \frac{E_{0}}{c} with c^{2} = \frac{1}{\mu_{0} \varepsilon_{0}} to show u_{E} = u_{B}
Since intensity is c times the total average energy density each field carries half the power
Hence the ratio of electric to magnetic contributions is 1 : 1
→ (A)

17947:
Recall the kinetic theory result for mean free path \lambda = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2} n \pi d^{2}}
Here n is the number density and d is the molecular diameter with collision cross section \pi d^{2}
Note the \sqrt{2} factor comes from relative motion of molecules in the derivation
Check dimensions since n d^{2} has units of inverse length as required for \lambda
This formula exactly matches the stated option
→ (A)

17948:
Apply mass energy equivalence E = m c^{2} with c = 3 \times 10^{8} m s^{-1}
Convert mass m = 0.5 g = 0.5 \times 10^{-3} kg = 5 \times 10^{-4} kg
Substitute E = 5 \times 10^{-4} \times (3 \times 10^{8})^{2}
Compute (3 \times 10^{8})^{2} = 9 \times 10^{16} so E = 45 \times 10^{12} J
Rewrite as 4.5 \times 10^{13} J which is the given option
→ (A)

17951:
Use the ideal gas law pV = nRT with n = \frac{m}{M} to get density \rho = \frac{pM}{RT}
Take molar mass of hydrogen H_{2} as M = 2 \times 10^{-3} kg mol^{-1}
Convert p = 249 kPa = 249 \times 10^{3} Pa and T = 27 + 273 = 300 K
Substitute \rho = \frac{249 \times 10^{3} \times 2 \times 10^{-3}}{8.3 \times 300} = \frac{498}{2490}
Evaluate to \rho = 0.2 kg m^{-3} which is the given option
→ (A)

17953:
Recall mobility defined as drift velocity per unit field \mu = \frac{v_{d}}{E}
Insert drift velocity v_{d} = 7.5 \times 10^{-4} m s^{-1}
Insert field E = 3 \times 10^{-10} V m^{-1}
Compute \mu = \frac{7.5 \times 10^{-4}}{3 \times 10^{-10}} = 2.5 \times 10^{6}
Attach units m^{2} V^{-1} s^{-1} to match the stated option
→ (A)

17955:
Recall permeability and susceptibility are linked by \mu = \mu_{0}(1 + \chi)
Here susceptibility is \chi = 599 so the bracket becomes 1 + 599 = 600
Substitute \mu_{0} = 4\pi \times 10^{-7} to get \mu = 600 \times 4\pi \times 10^{-7}
Compute 600 \times 4\pi \times 10^{-7} = 2400\pi \times 10^{-7} = 2.4\pi \times 10^{-4} \text{ T m A}^{-1}
This value matches the listed option → (D)

17956:
Recall a uniformly charged sphere behaves as a point charge for outside points
Here radius is 10 \text{ cm} and the point is at r = 15 \text{ cm} = 0.15 \text{ m}, so use E = \frac{1}{4\pi \varepsilon_{0}} \frac{Q}{r^{2}}
Substitute Q = 3.2 \times 10^{-7} \text{ C} and constant 9 \times 10^{9} to get E = \frac{9 \times 10^{9} \times 3.2 \times 10^{-7}}{(0.15)^{2}}
Compute numerator 2880 divided by 0.0225 gives 1.28 \times 10^{5}
Hence field is 1.28 \times 10^{5} \text{ N/C} → (A)

17957:
Recall phase angle satisfies \tan \phi = \frac{X}{R} with net reactance X
With L removed the circuit is RC and given \phi = \frac{\pi}{3} so \frac{X_{C}}{R} = \tan \frac{\pi}{3}
With C removed the circuit is RL and again \phi = \frac{\pi}{3} so \frac{X_{L}}{R} = \tan \frac{\pi}{3}
Thus X_{L} = X_{C} which is the resonance condition with zero net phase
At resonance power factor is \cos 0 = 1.0 → (B)

17958:
Recall capillary rise is h = \frac{2T \cos \theta}{r \rho g}
Mass held is m = \rho \pi r^{2} h which on substitution gives m = \frac{2\pi T \cos \theta}{g} \times r
Thus for the same liquid and tube material mass is proportional to radius with m \propto r
Doubling radius from r to 2r doubles the mass from 5 \text{ g} to 10.0 \text{ g}
Hence the wider tube holds 10.0 \text{ g} → (B)

17960:
Recall the upper input passes through a NOT gate to give \bar{A} and the lower input gives \bar{B}
Both signals enter a NOR gate whose output is Y = \overline{\bar{A} + \bar{B}}
Apply De Morgan law to simplify \overline{\bar{A} + \bar{B}} = A \cdot B which is AND operation
Truth values are 0 for 00, 01, 10 and 1 only for 11
That pattern matches the table in the listed option → (D)

17962:
Recall dielectric constant is K = \frac{C}{C_{0}}
Here C_{0} = 6 \text{ }\mu\text{F} and C = 30 \text{ }\mu\text{F} so K = \frac{30}{6} = 5
Permittivity follows \varepsilon = K \varepsilon_{0} with \varepsilon_{0} = 8.85 \times 10^{-12}
Compute 5 \times 8.85 \times 10^{-12} = 44.25 \times 10^{-12} = 0.44 \times 10^{-10} \text{ C}^{2} \text{ N}^{-1} \text{ m}^{-2}
This matches the listed option → (B)

17964:
Recall gravity at height h is g_{h} = g\left(\frac{R}{R + h}\right)^{2}
Here h = \frac{R}{2} so the factor is \left(\frac{R}{1.5R}\right)^{2} = \frac{1}{2.25} = \frac{4}{9}
Weight scales the same way so W_{h} = 72 \times \frac{4}{9}
Compute 72 \times 4 divided by 9 gives 32 \text{ N}
Hence force at that height is 32 \text{ N} → (A)

17965:
Recall centre of mass from one end is x = \frac{m_{2}L}{m_{1} + m_{2}}
Here m_{1} = 5 \text{ kg}, m_{2} = 10 \text{ kg} and rod length L = 1 \text{ m}
Substitute to get x = \frac{10 \times 1}{5 + 10} = \frac{10}{15} = 0.667 \text{ m}
Convert to centimetres to get nearly 67 \text{ cm} from the 5 \text{ kg} mass → (B)

17968:
Recall telescope resolution limit is \theta = \frac{1.22\lambda}{d}
Here \lambda = 600 \text{ nm} = 600 \times 10^{-9} \text{ m} and diameter d = 2 \text{ m}
Substitute to get \theta = \frac{1.22 \times 600 \times 10^{-9}}{2}
Compute 1.22 \times 300 \times 10^{-9} = 366 \times 10^{-9} = 3.66 \times 10^{-7} \text{ rad} → (D)

17969:
Recall metre bridge balance gives \frac{R_{wire}}{10} = \frac{3}{2}
Solve to get R_{wire} = 15 \text{ }\Omega for the full 1.5 \text{ m} length
Length per ohm is therefore \frac{1.5}{15}
Compute to get 0.1 \text{ m} = 1.0 \times 10^{-1} \text{ m} → (A)

17970:
For a non-reflecting surface at normal incidence the entire incident radiant energy is absorbed
Total incident power is flux multiplied by exposed area
Using P = 20 \text{ W cm}^{-2} \times 20 \text{ cm}^{2} gives P = 400 \text{ W}
Energy received in time t is E = Pt with t = 1 \text{ min} = 60 \text{ s}
This gives E = 400 \times 60 = 24000 \text{ J} = 24 \times 10^{3} \text{ J}
→ (B)

17972:
In a purely capacitive ac circuit the rms current is I_{rms} = \frac{V_{rms}}{X_{C}}
Capacitive reactance is X_{C} = \frac{1}{\omega C} = \frac{1}{2\pi fC}
Using V_{rms} = 200 \text{ V}, f = 50 \text{ Hz} and C = 40 \times 10^{-6} \text{ F}
This gives I_{rms} = 200 \times 2\pi \times 50 \times 40 \times 10^{-6} \approx 2.51 \text{ A}
Nearest value is 2.5 \text{ A}
→ (B)

17973:
Stress is defined as restoring force per unit area of cross section
Dimensionally this is \frac{\text{force}}{\text{area}} = \frac{MLT^{-2}}{L^{2}}
Dividing the powers of length gives ML^{-1}T^{-2}
This is also the dimension of pressure and of elastic moduli
Hence the correct dimension set is ML^{-1}T^{-2}
→ (C)

17975:
Young modulus is longitudinal stress divided by longitudinal strain
Stress equals applied force per area, here \frac{Mg}{A}
Strain equals extension per original length, here \frac{L_{1} - L}{L}
Dividing stress by strain gives Y = \frac{Mg}{A} \times \frac{L}{L_{1} - L}
Simplifying yields Y = \frac{MgL}{A(L_{1} - L)}
→ (C)

17976:
Electric potential of a short dipole at distance r and angle \theta is V = \frac{1}{4\pi\varepsilon_{0}} \frac{p\cos\theta}{r^{2}}
Here p = 16 \times 10^{-9} \text{ C m}, r = 0.6 \text{ m} and \theta = 60^\circ
With \cos 60^\circ = 0.5 the numerator is 9 \times 10^{9} \times 16 \times 10^{-9} \times 0.5 = 72
Dividing by r^{2} = 0.36 gives V = \frac{72}{0.36} = 200 \text{ V}
→ (A)

17977:
Beat frequency equals the magnitude of the difference of the two frequencies
With f_{A} = 530 \text{ Hz} and beats of 6 \text{ Hz}, f_{B} is 524 \text{ Hz} or 536 \text{ Hz}
Lowering the tension of string B decreases its frequency
After the change the beat frequency rises to 7 \text{ Hz}, so f_{B} moved away from f_{A}
That is possible only if f_{B} started below f_{A}
Hence the original frequency of B was 524 \text{ Hz}
→ (A)

17978:
De Broglie wavelength of an electron accelerated through V is \lambda = \frac{1.227}{\sqrt{V}} \text{ nm}
Given wavelength is 1.227 \times 10^{-2} \text{ nm}
Equating gives \sqrt{V} = \frac{1.227}{1.227 \times 10^{-2}} = 100
Squaring yields V = 10^{4} \text{ V}
This is the stated potential difference
→ (C)

17980:
Energy in electron volt is obtained by dividing the joule value by e
Conversion uses 1 \text{ eV} = 1.6 \times 10^{-19} \text{ J}
Given bond energy E = 10^{-20} \text{ J}
Dividing gives E = \frac{10^{-20}}{1.6 \times 10^{-19}} \approx 0.0625 \text{ eV}
Nearest value is 0.06
→ (B)

18002:
Use the first order integrated rate law in logarithmic form
Time t equals \frac{2.303}{k} \log_{10} \frac{[A]_{0}}{[A]}
Here \frac{[A]_{0}}{[A]} equals \frac{2.0}{0.2} equals 10, so the log term equals 1
Substitute k equals 4.606 \times 10^{-3} s^{-1} to get t equals \frac{2.303}{4.606 \times 10^{-3}}
This evaluates to 500 s, the time for a tenfold fall, which is option B
→ (B)

18022:
Spontaneity follows \Delta_{r}G^{\ominus} = -RT \ln K_{C} for the reaction
Here K_{C} = 2 \times 10^{13} at T = 300 K with R = 8.314 J mol^{-1} K^{-1}
Substitution gives \Delta_{r}G^{\ominus} = -8.314 \times 300 \times \ln(2 \times 10^{13}) J mol^{-1}
The negative sign reflects the very large equilibrium constant
This expression matches the given option, which is option D
→ (D)
